{"conversation_id":"ecafe287-3175-492d-8b41-98dda10a8419","status":"SUCCESS","response":"### Root Cause\n\nIn [`pkg/cache.py`](file:///D:/Projects/subagents-mcp/bench/fixture_repo/pkg/cache.py#L4-L5), [`_key`](file:///D:/Projects/subagents-mcp/bench/fixture_repo/pkg/cache.py#L4-L5) computed the cache key as `tuple(spec.items())`. Because Python dictionaries preserve insertion order, two dictionaries with identical keys and values inserted in different orders (such as `spec_a = {\"region\": \"eu\", \"tier\": \"gold\"}` and `spec_b = {\"tier\": \"gold\", \"region\": \"eu\"}`) yielded different tuples:\n- `spec_a` generated `(('region', 'eu'), ('tier', 'gold'))`\n- `spec_b` generated `(('tier', 'gold'), ('region', 'eu'))`\n\nThis resulted in a cache miss when looking up `spec_b` in [`SpecCache.get`](file:///D:/Projects/subagents-mcp/bench/fixture_repo/pkg/cache.py#L17-L23).\n\n### Fix\n\nUpdated [`_key`](file:///D:/Projects/subagents-mcp/bench/fixture_repo/pkg/cache.py#L4-L5) in [`pkg/cache.py`](file:///D:/Projects/subagents-mcp/bench/fixture_repo/pkg/cache.py#L4-L5) to sort the dictionary items before constructing the tuple:\n\n```python\ndef _key(spec):\n    return tuple(sorted(spec.items()))\n```\n\n### Verification\n\nRan the test suite:\n- `pytest test_cache.py test_smoke.py`: all 4 tests passed.\n- No test files were modified.\n","duration_seconds":182.8181832,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":292187,"output_tokens":12035,"thinking_tokens":8914,"cache_read_tokens":651329,"total_tokens":304222}}
